#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151889 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/id #uuid "3a4c4912-e478-5332-b088-0df7ac4e8c42" :question/math-academy-id "e-6578" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7e533704-ef66-5bab-b151-83345bce320b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6865" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "c4c3ed32-67c8-56a3-9855-4bfa2696bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6866" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662d1d06-dd17-5de3-83ec-2cedc1760998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343806} {:answer/id #uuid "d949191f-9f46-55e9-9520-7e4e12abf55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343807} {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf3db7b-9abf-5af5-bcdf-e17ab7417764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343809} {:answer/id #uuid "23cca72b-25f8-5148-9741-215c615b35b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} :answer-field/id #uuid "24817143-3fcf-5599-bdca-2bb39117ba27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9075355b-7512-5362-8a9e-57357160d013" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c12f1c6a-7897-5050-955e-c30d6cb6868e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343813} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aed976-74f4-52eb-883e-d874f2e5b4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343814} {:answer/id #uuid "3783d887-6dde-593d-9216-b4737ee03743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343815} {:answer/id #uuid "72db357b-5e94-521b-98cb-2e27bb3c0ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343816} {:answer/id #uuid "ec152012-bf24-54f6-912c-92eb68bc4fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec152012-bf24-54f6-912c-92eb68bc4fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343817} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27e28cc-8bfa-530d-8835-f467eec06e85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67dbd2c9-c2b1-519d-a98b-db897636de6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131016" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation $4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab4bee3-410b-5d35-a169-2ad76e41fb2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343820} {:answer/id #uuid "0649ef2a-c509-51a3-90f0-709555e3334a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343821} {:answer/id #uuid "0123dcd1-151b-5e93-b33c-8455ee006a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343822} {:answer/id #uuid "7f88787e-12af-5ad0-bd43-09116993b631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343823} {:answer/id #uuid "d141c907-baeb-56b6-a4dc-73c8aaa2f339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0649ef2a-c509-51a3-90f0-709555e3334a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343821} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5047de-6ecd-5229-ad34-95182ad33319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e825fa-5929-5a6d-9ff6-6f8f75d69f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131019" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=x$\n- $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}$ $$\n- $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{2x}=x{e}^{x}({e}^{x})$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.  The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=(1+x){e}^{x}={e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad8b97e-924a-588f-ae21-578df2180d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343827} {:answer/id #uuid "2e641187-eb10-5851-8598-faedcd00898a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343828} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd4043-78d5-5143-90d7-14b147500f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343829} {:answer/id #uuid "370ef8d7-7a1c-5d5c-bf91-c561f2638815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343830} {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831} :answer-field/id #uuid "597072c7-e9da-5af9-b902-e0614d3bb99d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0ea85a-8646-5af0-8c5d-b95cfa962333" :question/math-academy-id "q-131032" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbce86d-0a5a-5adc-bcae-44c7b09f5e26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150241} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d9ca21-9523-5c06-addb-5674280f6b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150242} {:answer/id #uuid "ee16122c-46f7-5f52-8ace-adafa325ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150243} {:answer/id #uuid "2959754c-71bb-5b54-9dc7-21943f2e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150244} {:answer/id #uuid "543ff1d3-ca9f-56d5-9ed3-36d49a51cb19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2959754c-71bb-5b54-9dc7-21943f2e1dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150244} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a49e14-23b4-51b1-a7bc-2efefc74290d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03be4c64-335b-58c9-94d2-aea65ff76ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65077" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad19687-6e23-57cd-9ff3-9d4b4c3ce8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343834} {:answer/id #uuid "241b9848-c1d4-53bb-b118-7409afaed178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343835} {:answer/id #uuid "556dbf4a-04f8-5469-9f2d-90c8257c6e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343836} {:answer/id #uuid "93e58577-7311-5282-ba44-187ea3331398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343837} {:answer/id #uuid "46d17f69-8c9b-5e2b-8c62-6204d2f261e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241b9848-c1d4-53bb-b118-7409afaed178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343835} :answer-field/id #uuid "1331ebf1-82dc-531e-9e20-df835f5fc51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "565f81e0-6304-5ffd-8a7d-7b22ca693e78" :question/math-academy-id "q-65104" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59fedde1-1190-550a-8393-9961c437f471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343841} {:answer/id #uuid "f29fe753-5d61-5e46-b078-04aa0943561b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343842} {:answer/id #uuid "d7030cee-5c8c-53e0-8824-88c64db9547a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343843} {:answer/id #uuid "b7986287-bcab-5a19-bd59-cda4888ceab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343844} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1c97be-7f91-50fb-a911-0387caaa1907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1c97be-7f91-50fb-a911-0387caaa1907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343845} :answer-field/id #uuid "582f420d-e6cd-5466-971f-6caa16d216fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9172234-db56-51a9-9848-44f3d2002e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-65110" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+4y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ is **not** a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=1,{c}_{2}=5.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is **not** a solution of the given equation because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f604d8-2b33-5777-a6a0-37c712eac239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343848} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf937ba-0b08-56c6-81a6-a1fd6d1d24bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343849} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a517ca-f0bb-5ee0-917e-5190cdd14c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343850} {:answer/id #uuid "dfefa430-f27e-5503-a71b-acde25cf2308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}+2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343851} {:answer/id #uuid "71d20e4a-8766-5c1a-9a42-fbb22450821c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a517ca-f0bb-5ee0-917e-5190cdd14c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343850} :answer-field/id #uuid "214594fe-4e35-50cc-bf8d-46d6e10ec506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7843d91-66d7-5e8e-8e74-3ce1b90ca3da" :question/math-academy-id "q-65113" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{e}^{-3x}-{e}^{3x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed46b30c-1f0c-5457-9c44-574a8cbdbbf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343855} {:answer/id #uuid "e26a4325-a8bf-5731-9904-388c229204ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343856} {:answer/id #uuid "8afa32dd-1288-5351-9ae9-2498943892b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343857} {:answer/id #uuid "9f47b88e-2ff4-568f-983e-97c6643af732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343858} {:answer/id #uuid "8488c4f3-09e9-5aa4-acff-956eda98c33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8488c4f3-09e9-5aa4-acff-956eda98c33e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343859} :answer-field/id #uuid "c745f469-f6b2-55e5-a208-175a6ce7777f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fc2392e-650b-5c11-b372-7d113697609b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79770" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}+{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72c6e1c-d257-5ad1-93b5-6eb80bc738b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343862} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5a050-34cd-58d0-8449-b9dcabaf82d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343863} {:answer/id #uuid "07c8c2b6-a5e7-53a5-adb2-79404773427d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343864} {:answer/id #uuid "b5209a10-aa98-53b5-895d-ecce39b09eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3bfc3b-6379-5e8d-ba2c-71c72115635f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ccb0584-4fe2-575d-b3bf-456e92f83038" :question/math-academy-id "q-79774" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fcf40e8-db61-5b8a-89e2-7fbd81af67b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343869} {:answer/id #uuid "ad91eff9-fc9c-5a5f-b08f-d46ae4d25442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343870} {:answer/id #uuid "92761bd0-3be0-599c-9ce2-96c3b4724f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343871} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3189a2-d0e9-50a9-9abd-95d308d20f4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343872} {:answer/id #uuid "18a78e72-8d85-55c3-9201-d2b55225aec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fcf40e8-db61-5b8a-89e2-7fbd81af67b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343869} :answer-field/id #uuid "769cdea7-74dc-5d98-9a24-374603bc2336" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76c00a91-3d84-5b9d-a58e-5823a1a84973" :question/math-academy-id "q-79775" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$\n- $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2x{e}^{3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=6{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=6.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-5{e}^{3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca176903-13cf-5bf4-af73-b2a9bcd67b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343876} {:answer/id #uuid "908715c5-59eb-5841-b320-ccc51945869f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877} {:answer/id #uuid "e63988a8-aa36-50b6-8eae-d06943d1ebff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343878} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2bfa3f-3d48-57cd-9c33-2fb81fe223fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343879} {:answer/id #uuid "d06c4b24-f408-5547-8fb0-cf9bc089ccae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "908715c5-59eb-5841-b320-ccc51945869f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877} :answer-field/id #uuid "7341eb2a-4f83-553b-87f4-f8e8797f52dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba996a65-1416-5958-94e3-22846cd2f798" :question/math-academy-id "q-79777" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130220bd-a703-5d54-82df-bca1be744b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343883} {:answer/id #uuid "010b74ca-d8d8-563f-bf55-0b92d3905dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343884} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e1315b-2e79-5500-93da-2bd04bb80dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343885} {:answer/id #uuid "48b3ce09-1104-5a9f-98e4-449eb312f874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{3t}+5{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343886} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ad84e-5778-543d-883d-c5aa940ac6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{t}-{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "010b74ca-d8d8-563f-bf55-0b92d3905dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2+3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343884} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd7281a-23d6-5fc0-972f-c637ee1479b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9eb5285-820b-55f8-947d-d2de2fae6b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-79797" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{5t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}(t)-8{y}^{′}(t)+15y(t)=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{5t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}={e}^{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3t}={e}^{2+3t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9e8daf-3b95-5a60-bdb0-85d9e86ba4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343890} {:answer/id #uuid "cbddd149-3077-5012-84bf-e1799524c4bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343891} {:answer/id #uuid "07338c9a-efd0-59bb-a0ad-c7ffc2db516f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343892} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e1aa36-60bc-59d8-b000-51261b8c6349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343893} {:answer/id #uuid "79c5ffc1-f12f-5e0f-a0b8-1b58d24ccc54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07338c9a-efd0-59bb-a0ad-c7ffc2db516f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343892} :answer-field/id #uuid "838633f3-25f7-5014-8095-87e91b2aac41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0cfcfe2-84f9-5311-a08d-2870048acdde" :question/math-academy-id "q-79832" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-5}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}-5y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$\n- $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=5{x}^{-4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={x}^{-4}$ is not a solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{x}^{-5}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c79de54-5932-55eb-a8fe-9801c2c1da3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343897} {:answer/id #uuid "5437ad54-cce2-5bb6-b968-c705d2da81ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343898} {:answer/id #uuid "670a93d9-7f58-57b0-a015-70980bddf3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343899} {:answer/id #uuid "66f0a1c2-5a2b-53c1-a093-50382b646b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343900} {:answer/id #uuid "75a487b3-e8fe-54ab-aea5-9ccb506cab1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66f0a1c2-5a2b-53c1-a093-50382b646b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343900} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6bd62b-df23-51e5-b23f-206ef2f96baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cde2b3d5-2be9-59bb-97d6-03589a9f5f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79833" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-6x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}$\n- $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{3-6x}={e}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{-6x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=-{e}^{6x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $y={e}^{6x}$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
